{
  "wireMagic": "pb3",
  "pens": [ {
  
  } ],
  "wsWinStyles": [ {
  
  } ],
  "wpWinPositions": [ {
  
  } ],
  "events": [ {
    "tStartMs": 269,
    "dDurationMs": 3261,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Thank you to Blinkist for supporting PBS."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 3530,
    "dDurationMs": 4050,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "How do you see the unseeable - how do you\nexplore the inescapable?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 7580,
    "dDurationMs": 4580,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Our cleverest astronomers have figured out\nways to catch light that skims the very edge"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 12160,
    "dDurationMs": 976,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of black holes."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 13136,
    "dDurationMs": 2067,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Let’s find out what they learned."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 20250,
    "dDurationMs": 4770,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "A few weeks ago a story made the rounds of pop-sci media proclaiming that for the first"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 25020,
    "dDurationMs": 3720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "time light had been detected from behind a black hole."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 28740,
    "dDurationMs": 5470,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The reports were about a paper that claimed to have seen X-rays that came not from inside"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 34210,
    "dDurationMs": 2510,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "but from the back end black hole."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 36720,
    "dDurationMs": 4560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "This is obviously cool stuff - I mean, really\nanything new with black holes captures the"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 41280,
    "dDurationMs": 1000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "public attention."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 42280,
    "dDurationMs": 4210,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But this result is cool in ways most people\naren’t aware."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 46490,
    "dDurationMs": 4479,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And it brought us closer to a goal that I’ve\nbeen personally striving towards for years"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 50969,
    "dDurationMs": 4681,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "- trying to understand what happens in the\nvicinity of the largest black holes in the"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 55650,
    "dDurationMs": 1000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "universe."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 56650,
    "dDurationMs": 5220,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "I thought it would be worth doing a space\ntime journal club on the Nature paper by Wilkins,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 61870,
    "dDurationMs": 5490,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Gallo, Costantini, Brandt \u0026 Blandford so I\nget to talk about my favorite subject in the"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 67360,
    "dDurationMs": 1000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "universe."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 68360,
    "dDurationMs": 4920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Now you might remember the picture of the\nblack hole captured by the event horizon telescope."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 73280,
    "dDurationMs": 4560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "We talked about this soon after it came out\n- but to remind you, here we have radio light"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 77840,
    "dDurationMs": 3709,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "from charged particles whirling around the\nblack hole."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 81549,
    "dDurationMs": 5180,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And the more recent version of this in polarized\nlight shows the grain of the magnetic field"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 86729,
    "dDurationMs": 1960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "right near the black hole’s edge."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 88689,
    "dDurationMs": 4890,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "This image was produced through a herculean effort - a team of hundreds of scientists"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 93579,
    "dDurationMs": 4650,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and engineers synthesizing data from radio\ntelescopes across the globe."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 98229,
    "dDurationMs": 4420,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "They stitched the image together using the\nvery latest machine learning techniques to"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 102649,
    "dDurationMs": 4731,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "achieve an image resolution equivalent to\na telescope the size of the planet earth."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 107380,
    "dDurationMs": 2129,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "All very impressive, very state-of-the-art."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 109509,
    "dDurationMs": 5360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But the thing that I love about the result\nwe’re discussing today is its simple elegance."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 114869,
    "dDurationMs": 5130,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "We’ve been using the same technique for\ndecades to understand quasars."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 119999,
    "dDurationMs": 4311,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "This method doesn’t need an international\nteam or a planet-sized telescope - it can"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 124310,
    "dDurationMs": 4589,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "be done with a single, ordinary scope, and\none astronomer with lots of patience."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 128899,
    "dDurationMs": 5401,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "That technique is reverberation mapping, in\nwhich we watch as a flare of light from a"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 134300,
    "dDurationMs": 5990,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "violent event near the black hole radiates\nand reflects - reverberates - its way through"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 140290,
    "dDurationMs": 3330,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the complex structure of a quasar."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 143620,
    "dDurationMs": 5140,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "When that light reaches us - many, many millions of light years away - it’s still imprinted with"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 148760,
    "dDurationMs": 4040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "a wealth of information about the monster\nthat it escaped."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 152800,
    "dDurationMs": 4180,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Reverberation mapping is all about untangling\nthat information."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 156980,
    "dDurationMs": 4720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "We do this by watching how the spectrum of the quasar changes over time."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 161700,
    "dDurationMs": 5176,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "A spectrum, by the way, is what you get when you split light into its component colors"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 166876,
    "dDurationMs": 1364,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "or wavelengths."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 168240,
    "dDurationMs": 5160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So basically you watch the quasar, which is\njust a faint point of light on the sky, and"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 173400,
    "dDurationMs": 6370,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "stretch it into an even fainter flickering\nrainbow, and then try to pull from that full"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 179770,
    "dDurationMs": 1750,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "3-D map of the quasar."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 181520,
    "dDurationMs": 4160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "To understand exactly how astrophysicists\ndo this, I’m going to take you on a journey"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 185680,
    "dDurationMs": 5380,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "- we’re going to follow a flare from its\norigin deep in the belly of the beast - uncomfortably"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 191060,
    "dDurationMs": 2680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "close to the supermassive black hole."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 193740,
    "dDurationMs": 5180,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Supermassive, by the way, is the actual technical term for the largest black holes in the universe"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 198920,
    "dDurationMs": 3800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "- anything more than a million or so times\nthe mass of the Sun."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 202720,
    "dDurationMs": 4490,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "These things are pretty much only found in\nthe very centers of galaxies, and pretty much"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 207210,
    "dDurationMs": 3900,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "every decent sized galaxy has one of these\nat their center."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 211110,
    "dDurationMs": 5250,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "They are mostly dark, except when a bunch of gas shows up in the galactic core - then"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 216360,
    "dDurationMs": 5220,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "you get this gigantic whirlpool of searing\nplasma screaming around the black hole."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 221580,
    "dDurationMs": 4239,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "AKA an accretion disk, and switching on the\nquasar"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 225819,
    "dDurationMs": 2511,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Quasars are not what you’d call stable."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 228330,
    "dDurationMs": 4900,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "They sputter and flare with violent events,\nfor example as dense clumps of gas hitting"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 233230,
    "dDurationMs": 4490,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the center, or magnetic instabilities shaking\nthings up."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 237720,
    "dDurationMs": 5270,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "However it happens, when a flare is generated near the black hole it expands outwards, and"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 242990,
    "dDurationMs": 2390,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "it causes a lot of trouble on the way."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 245380,
    "dDurationMs": 4290,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The expanding shell first rips across the\nsurface of the accretion disk, causing different"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 249670,
    "dDurationMs": 4690,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "parts of the disk to brighten - first the\nshorter wavelength which corresponds to the"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 254360,
    "dDurationMs": 5730,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "hot, inner disk, then to longer wavelengths\nof the cooler, outer regions."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 260090,
    "dDurationMs": 2859,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The flare then continues outwards and upwards."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 262949,
    "dDurationMs": 5060,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Although it’s energetic, the accretion disk\nis relatively well behaved - at least compared"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 268009,
    "dDurationMs": 2660,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to the wild all stuff around it."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 270669,
    "dDurationMs": 6431,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "A maelstrom of lower density gas rages above and below and beyond the accretion disk."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 277100,
    "dDurationMs": 4289,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "That gas is accelerated by a combination of\nthe incredible gravitational field of the"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 281389,
    "dDurationMs": 5111,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "black hole and the continuous blaze of high\nenergy radiation from the inner accretion"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 286500,
    "dDurationMs": 1060,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "disk."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 287560,
    "dDurationMs": 3710,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "It can be driven to up to 10% or more of the\nspeed of light."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 291270,
    "dDurationMs": 5869,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Pockets of gas within this flow are somehow shielded and so that gas can cool down a bit."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 297139,
    "dDurationMs": 4840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "This gas starts to glow in a different way\n- not from heat, but from the motion of electrons"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 301979,
    "dDurationMs": 2671,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "between their atomic energy levels."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 304650,
    "dDurationMs": 5159,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "This allows the maelstrom to reprocess some of the light of the accretion disk so that"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 309809,
    "dDurationMs": 5260,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "it shines bright in very specific colors depending on what atoms are present."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 315069,
    "dDurationMs": 4961,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "In a normal spectrum we see the light from these electron transitions as sharp spikes"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 320030,
    "dDurationMs": 2659,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "at specific wavelengths - what we call emission lines."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 322689,
    "dDurationMs": 5182,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But in a quasar, the gas is moving fast, and\nthat motion shifts the wavelengths of the"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 327871,
    "dDurationMs": 1358,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "light as we see it."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 329229,
    "dDurationMs": 6090,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Now we’ve all experienced this Doppler shift\nwhen the sound waves of an ambulance siren"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 335319,
    "dDurationMs": 4831,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "shift between higher and lower pitch as it\npasses us on the street."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 340150,
    "dDurationMs": 4280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "In the case of the quasar’s emission lines,\ngas is moving towards us and away from us"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 344430,
    "dDurationMs": 1759,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "at many different speeds."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 346189,
    "dDurationMs": 4320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Sharp emission lines get shifted to many different degrees add together to produce the classic"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 350509,
    "dDurationMs": 4660,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "broad emission lines - perhaps the most striking feature of the quasar spectrum, and one that"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 355169,
    "dDurationMs": 3400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "confused the hell out of the astronomers who first saw them."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 358569,
    "dDurationMs": 4021,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And, honestly, it’s been confusing us ever\nsince."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 362590,
    "dDurationMs": 2990,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "From the Doppler shift we can tell that the\ngas is moving fast."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 365580,
    "dDurationMs": 1910,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But that doesn’t tell us what drives the\ngas."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 367490,
    "dDurationMs": 3209,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Is this stuff pouring towards the black hole?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 370699,
    "dDurationMs": 1000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Orbiting it?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 371699,
    "dDurationMs": 4231,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Or being blasted out by the crazy radiation\nfrom the accretion disk?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 375930,
    "dDurationMs": 3859,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Now this is where reverberating mapping can be the most powerful."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 379789,
    "dDurationMs": 4201,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The light from this gas is ultimately powered by the light from the accretion disk."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 383990,
    "dDurationMs": 5729,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So when our expanding shell of light rips\nthrough this gas the broad lines get supercharged"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 389719,
    "dDurationMs": 2771,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "- but in a complicated way."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 392490,
    "dDurationMs": 3019,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "First the gas near the center brightens, then\nthe gas further out."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 395509,
    "dDurationMs": 4530,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "At the same time, the response of the gas\non the far side of the quasar is slower than"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 400039,
    "dDurationMs": 5190,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the gas of near side because the far-side’s light has to travel further to get to us."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 405229,
    "dDurationMs": 2750,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Let’s look at a simplistic example."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 407979,
    "dDurationMs": 4090,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Two totally different scenarios for how gas\nmight be moving."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 412069,
    "dDurationMs": 4520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "One - it’s pouring in, rivers of gas dragged\ndown by the black hole’s gravity."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 416589,
    "dDurationMs": 4711,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And two: it’s being blasted out, riding\nthe flow of radiation from the inner accretion"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 421300,
    "dDurationMs": 1000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "disk."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 422300,
    "dDurationMs": 4380,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Either of these scenarios could produce the\nsame Doppler-broadened emission lines."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 426680,
    "dDurationMs": 3180,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But reverberation mapping can tell between\nthem."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 429860,
    "dDurationMs": 6259,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Consider case one: if gas is pouring in then\nit’s accelerating - moving fastest towards"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 436119,
    "dDurationMs": 1181,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the center."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 437300,
    "dDurationMs": 5049,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "This means this extreme velocity gas - the\nfar ends of the broad line - should respond"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 442349,
    "dDurationMs": 3240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "first as our flare reverberates out."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 445589,
    "dDurationMs": 5211,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Also, in this scenario the gas on the far\nside of the black hole is moving towards the"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 450800,
    "dDurationMs": 2410,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "black hole and so towards us."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 453210,
    "dDurationMs": 2949,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Its light is blue-shifted to shorter wavelengths."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 456159,
    "dDurationMs": 5730,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "That gas responds late to the flare because\nthe light has to take this roundabout journey."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 461889,
    "dDurationMs": 4471,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Meanwhile the gas closer to us is actually\nmoving away from us as it falls towards the"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 466360,
    "dDurationMs": 3559,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "black hole - it’s redshifted to longer wavelengths."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 469919,
    "dDurationMs": 2641,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And it responds before the blue-shifted light."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 472560,
    "dDurationMs": 5289,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So in the in-flow model, the quickest response is for the red side of the emission line,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 477849,
    "dDurationMs": 1720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the slowest for the blue side"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 479569,
    "dDurationMs": 4451,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "It’s exactly the opposite for case two - if\ngas is being blasted out."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 484020,
    "dDurationMs": 5639,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Then the gas accelerates outwards, so we expect the faster moving gas to respond later than"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 489659,
    "dDurationMs": 1690,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the slow gas."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 491349,
    "dDurationMs": 4910,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And now the gas on the far side of the black\nhole is moving away while the gas on our side"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 496259,
    "dDurationMs": 4680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "is moving towards us - so the redshifted gas responds after the blueshifted gas."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 500939,
    "dDurationMs": 6250,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Oh, and I forgot the third option: maybe the\ngas is swirling around in a giant tornado"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 507189,
    "dDurationMs": 1741,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "above the accretion disk."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 508930,
    "dDurationMs": 6120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "In that case inner gas is moving faster like\nin the infall case, but now the red and blue"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 515050,
    "dDurationMs": 3520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "sides should respond at roughly the same time."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 518570,
    "dDurationMs": 4230,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Reverberation mapping has helped us tell between these three - well sort of, anyway."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 522800,
    "dDurationMs": 4520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "It turns out that all three types of motion\nare seen in different quasars."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 527320,
    "dDurationMs": 5060,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The emerging picture is that gas falls in\nalong some trajectories, but then gets lifted"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 532380,
    "dDurationMs": 6530,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "off the accretion disk by the blazing radiation of the inner disk into a vortex and then is"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 538910,
    "dDurationMs": 1120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "blasted outwards."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 540030,
    "dDurationMs": 3950,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "That’s about as far as we’ve got with\nthe broad-line tornado."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 543980,
    "dDurationMs": 5440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "It’s really, really hard to do this stuff\nbecause it takes years for big, powerful quasars"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 549420,
    "dDurationMs": 2510,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to finish responding to a flare in the core."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 551930,
    "dDurationMs": 5460,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Over the next decade things will get better\nas new telescopes like the Vera Rubin Observatory"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 557390,
    "dDurationMs": 1000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "come online."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 558390,
    "dDurationMs": 4750,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "We’ll be able to watch millions of quasars\nall do their thing at the same time."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 563140,
    "dDurationMs": 4870,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "OK, it’s time we got back to the discovery\nthat started our little journey - the light"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 568010,
    "dDurationMs": 3140,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "that was detected from behind a black hole."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 571150,
    "dDurationMs": 6080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The events described in the paper happened extremely close to a relatively nearby supermassive"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 577230,
    "dDurationMs": 1160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "black hole."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 578390,
    "dDurationMs": 6750,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The object is I Zwicky 1 - a so-called Seyfert\ngalaxy, which is like a mini-quasar - this"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 585140,
    "dDurationMs": 2300,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "one around 100 million light years away."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 587440,
    "dDurationMs": 4480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Near the black hole there are sources of light that I haven’t mentioned yet."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 591920,
    "dDurationMs": 5100,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The intense radiation in this region strips\nalmost all atoms of their electrons."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 597020,
    "dDurationMs": 4710,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "That leads to a haze of high-energy electrons surrounding the black hole."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 601730,
    "dDurationMs": 5160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "As light from the accretion disk passes through this haze it gains energy from the electrons,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 606890,
    "dDurationMs": 2770,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "boosting it all the way up to X-ray energies."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 609660,
    "dDurationMs": 3300,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "This is the X-ray corona."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 612960,
    "dDurationMs": 4860,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "We also have winds of matter flowing at incredible speeds - but this close to the black hole"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 617820,
    "dDurationMs": 5100,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "only the heaviest elements like iron can hold on to any of their electrons."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 622920,
    "dDurationMs": 7912,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "We see that iron because it shines at a specific X-ray wavelength - this is the iron K-alpha line."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 630832,
    "dDurationMs": 2428,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So here’s what happened."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 633260,
    "dDurationMs": 5910,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The X-ray corona flared bright, probably in\na small region right above the black hole."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 639170,
    "dDurationMs": 4550,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "That light then spread in all directions - some traveled straight to us, some reflected off"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 643720,
    "dDurationMs": 5000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the disk in our direction, but some also reflected off the disk on the opposite side of the black"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 648720,
    "dDurationMs": 1130,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "hole."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 649850,
    "dDurationMs": 4720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "A portion of that light was then grabbed by\nthe black hole’s gravitational field and"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 654570,
    "dDurationMs": 4390,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "slung right back around towards us, and magnified in the process."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 658960,
    "dDurationMs": 3680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "It was as we say, gravitationally lensed."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 662640,
    "dDurationMs": 4990,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "These three paths were apparent in the three distinct phases of the flare."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 667630,
    "dDurationMs": 4370,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "They were also able to see there was a difference in the response of the different parts of"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 672000,
    "dDurationMs": 1300,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the iron line."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 673300,
    "dDurationMs": 5570,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The blue side varied before the red side - and we talked about how this suggests an outflow."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 678870,
    "dDurationMs": 6960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "We have other reasons to think that this iron-laced wind also has a powerful rotational component."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 685830,
    "dDurationMs": 5300,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So it looks like we’re seeing an expanding\nvortex of stuff that just narrowly escaped"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 691130,
    "dDurationMs": 1890,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "being sucked into the black hogle."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 693020,
    "dDurationMs": 4370,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The paper goes into a lot more detail than\nthis - lots of good stuff like measuring the"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 697390,
    "dDurationMs": 5840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "mass of the black hole - 30 million Suns - to\nverifying Einstein’s general theory of relativity."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 703230,
    "dDurationMs": 3880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So you can check out the links in the description for more details."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 707110,
    "dDurationMs": 5600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "After decades of practice, and inventing better and better telescopes, we’re starting to"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 712710,
    "dDurationMs": 1410,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "get good at this game."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 714120,
    "dDurationMs": 5390,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "I just love it when we can map the space around black holes by watching flickering points"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 719510,
    "dDurationMs": 6430,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "in the sky, and in that flickering reconstruct\nhow light reverberates around the most extreme"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 725940,
    "dDurationMs": 2040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "regions of space time."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 727980,
    "dDurationMs": 2880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "We’d like to thank Blinkist for supporting\nPBS."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 730860,
    "dDurationMs": 4730,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "It can be hard to find the time to sit down\nand read a whole book, but now you can in"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 735590,
    "dDurationMs": 1090,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the blink of an eye."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 736680,
    "dDurationMs": 4570,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Blinkist is an app that produces key take\naways from thousands of nonfiction books and"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 741250,
    "dDurationMs": 5410,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "condenses them into 15 minutes that you can either read or listen to."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 746660,
    "dDurationMs": 4060,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Blinkist has an array of categories – from\nthe science of Quantum Mechanics to philosophy"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 750720,
    "dDurationMs": 1390,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to futurism."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 752110,
    "dDurationMs": 5380,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "For more information and a Blinkist limited\ntrial, please go to the link in the description."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 757490,
    "dDurationMs": 4660,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Today we’re covering comments from the last two episodes: first was the Everett-Wheeler"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 762150,
    "dDurationMs": 4530,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "telephone, where we explored how to send messages across the quantum multiverse."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 766680,
    "dDurationMs": 5350,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And then the episode on how vacuum decay could destroy the universe .. or universes, if the"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 772030,
    "dDurationMs": 2330,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "ideas in the first episode are right."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 774360,
    "dDurationMs": 4200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Mark V-A wonders whether faster than light\ncommunication methods might be detectable"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 778560,
    "dDurationMs": 5020,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "from great distances - as in, could we see\nthe effects of FTL communication from distant"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 783580,
    "dDurationMs": 1750,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "alien civilizations?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 785330,
    "dDurationMs": 6220,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Well, warp fields are supposed to blast an\nintense beam of radiation ahead of them when"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 791550,
    "dDurationMs": 5400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "they come out of warp, which could potentially be seen if they were pointed at us."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 796950,
    "dDurationMs": 4490,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Some people have proposed that this is what gamma ray bursts might be."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 801440,
    "dDurationMs": 4580,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Those people are dead wrong because it would mean the number of FTL-capable civilizations"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 806020,
    "dDurationMs": 4960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "in the galaxy would have to be enormous for the numbers to work out."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 810980,
    "dDurationMs": 3350,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "That’s for actual FTL travel."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 814330,
    "dDurationMs": 5900,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "For communication the warp bubble can be much much smaller, so probably wouldn’t be able"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 820230,
    "dDurationMs": 1860,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to observe those."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 822090,
    "dDurationMs": 2430,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Same with entanglement-based communication."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 824520,
    "dDurationMs": 5790,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The way we’d most likely detect FTL activity\nis because FTL-capable civilizations shouldn’t"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 830310,
    "dDurationMs": 5260,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "have any problem coming to visit us.- which\nthey haven’t, so there’s some evidence"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 835570,
    "dDurationMs": 1000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "right there."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 836570,
    "dDurationMs": 6350,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Karina makes a similar style of argument - saying that this crazy idea just makes it even more"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 842920,
    "dDurationMs": 4570,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "convincing that Schrodinger's equation is\nlinear."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 847490,
    "dDurationMs": 5840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "In other words, if the non-linearity of the\nschrodinger equation allows these paradox-generating"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 853330,
    "dDurationMs": 5920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "phenomena, then we can take that as evidence against that non-linearity."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 859250,
    "dDurationMs": 4870,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And honestly, I think that’s the most useful\ntakeaway from Polchinski and Weinberg’s"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 864120,
    "dDurationMs": 1000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "work."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 865120,
    "dDurationMs": 6150,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Moritz von Schweinitz and TBE_pryce both commented that the vacuum decay scenario sounds a lot"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 871270,
    "dDurationMs": 6850,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "like the big bang - what with it producing\nan expanding bubble full of energetic particles."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 878120,
    "dDurationMs": 1050,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Excellent observation."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 879170,
    "dDurationMs": 5780,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "In fact cosmic inflation - the event that\nmany physicists put the bang in the big bang"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 884950,
    "dDurationMs": 2840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "- IS a type of vacuum decay."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 887790,
    "dDurationMs": 4030,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Some scalar field that may or may not have\nbeen the Higgs field, is thought to have dropped"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 891820,
    "dDurationMs": 2410,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "from a much higher value into a stable minimum."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 894230,
    "dDurationMs": 6380,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The difference is that in the pre-inflation\nuniverse, space was expanding exponentially"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 900610,
    "dDurationMs": 2530,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "quickly due to the high value of that field."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 903140,
    "dDurationMs": 6220,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "That means a new vacuum decay event now wouldn’t\nyield universe whose interior is expanding"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 909360,
    "dDurationMs": 5430,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "quickly - in the sense of particles racing\napart from each other. You can look at our past episodes"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 914790,
    "dDurationMs": 2590,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "on inflation for all the gory details"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 917380,
    "dDurationMs": 5320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Dr Patrick Bryant from Carnegie Mellon University popped into the comments section and told us a little"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 922700,
    "dDurationMs": 6090,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "about his work using the large hadron collider to try to map the shape of the Higgs potential"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 928790,
    "dDurationMs": 1970,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "by looking at Higgs boson pair production."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 930760,
    "dDurationMs": 4160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Dr Bryant’s answered one question that I\nalso had."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 934920,
    "dDurationMs": 4190,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "How big does the vacuum decay bubble need to be in order to propagate."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 939110,
    "dDurationMs": 3600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "If it’s too small and it’ll collapse on\nitself."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 942710,
    "dDurationMs": 5280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Actually, Dr Bryant didn’t know the full answer - but he tells us that it depends on the energy"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 947990,
    "dDurationMs": 4640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "difference between the false and true vacuum\n- presumably the bigger the difference the"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 952630,
    "dDurationMs": 1470,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "smaller the bubble needs to be."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 954100,
    "dDurationMs": 4730,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But I would also imagine that the bigger the\nenergy difference, the less probability of"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 958830,
    "dDurationMs": 4430,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the field tunneling to the true minimum, so\nmaybe those cancel out?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 963260,
    "dDurationMs": 2330,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "I’m just making stuff up now."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 965590,
    "dDurationMs": 4410,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Dr Bryant, if you wouldn't mind hurrying up\nwith mapping the Higgs potential so we know"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 970000,
    "dDurationMs": 2950,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the answer to this, that’d be great."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 972950,
    "dDurationMs": 3410,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "You mention you probably need a bigger collider to do that properly."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 976360,
    "dDurationMs": 2120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Cool. Let’s do it."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 978480,
    "dDurationMs": 5140,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "AAALE quips \"If we figure out how to send\nmessages backwards in time, I'll make sure I'm"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 983620,
    "dDurationMs": 2130,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the first to know.”"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 985750,
    "dDurationMs": 5100,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "If I figure out how to send messages backwards in time I’ll warn myself not to do video"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 990850,
    "dDurationMs": 5240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "intros in which I say I’m going to send\nmessages between universes … in a Scottish"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 996090,
    "dDurationMs": 1680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "accent."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 997770,
    "dDurationMs": 4020,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Mostly because everyone thought it was a Russian accent - except the Scots, who just thought"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1001790,
    "dDurationMs": 2450,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "it was really bad."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1004240,
    "dDurationMs": 4980,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But to that I answer - you people have no idea what Scottish accents sound like in parallel"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1009220,
    "dDurationMs": 1500,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "universes."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1010720,
    "dDurationMs": 5884,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "That accent was as unfalsifiable as the Many Worlds interpretation, so there."
    } ]
  } ]
}
